ID: B3-Ch11a-Intro
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch11_diane,0)
set(v_b3_ch11_escape,0)
set(v_b3_ch11_friend,0)
set(v_b3_ch11_hear,0)
set(v_b3_ch11_magic,0)
set(v_b3_ch11_melindra,0)
set(v_b3_ch11_mocking,0)
set(v_b3_ch11_money,0)
set(v_b3_ch11_path,0)
set(v_b3_ch11_puzzle4,0)
set(v_b3_ch11_red,0)
set(v_b3_ch11_reverse,0)
set(v_b3_ch11_transferred,0)
set(v_b3_ch11_weakness,0)
set(v_is_dead,0)
 

"Let me see if I have this straight," Hadrik says, planting both fists on the heavy stone slab between them and leaning across until his beard brushes the map. "You people have been siphoning energy from every unsuspecting mage across this continent since the day the tournament opened, all to set an ambush for Eiden."

"Yes," Meridith says without blinking. Her posture remains rigid, hands resting flat against the polished seredium table.

"And if your grand machine fails to turn him to ash," Hadrik continues, his voice dropping into a dangerous rumble, "you want us to step in as your butchers. Cut down a crippled, half-drained man after you've bled him dry from the shadows. That's the bargain?"

"That is... an uncharitable framing," Meridith replies, her chin lifting slightly. "But stripped of sentiment, yes. That is the necessity."

"Do you have no honor at all?" Hadrik's lip curls in unfiltered disgust.

"Perhaps there has been a misunderstanding of terms," Meridith says, shifting back a fraction of an inch as the stone beneath her fingers hums with faint tension. "If you would permit me to lay out the strategic calculus once more, I am confident we can reach an accommodation—"

"No. I've heard quite enough." Hadrik shoves his chair back. The heavy stone legs screech violently against the seredium floor, echoing through the chamber like a cracked bell.

Diane tenses instantly, her hand dropping toward her belt as she rises, but Meridith snaps an arm across her chest, pinning her in place with a sharp shake of her head.

"You and your burrowing conspirators can scurry through the dirt with your traps all you like," Hadrik says, spitting the words onto the stone. "Find another butcher for your coward's work. I'm done."

He turns on his heel and strides out into the corridor, his thick shoulders rigid with fury, not giving the room a second glance.

"Hadrik! Hey—wait up!" Daren calls out, rising so fast his breastplate clatters against the table edge. The only answer is the heavy, resonant thud of dwarven boots receding down the resonant seredium hall.

"I need to go after him," Daren says, turning a stern look on Meridith. "The last thing we need is him blundering into one of your rival factions while his blood is up. I'll bring him back once he's cooled off."

"There is no need to rush," Meridith says, smoothing the front of her robes with deliberate care as she reclaims her composure. "We will reconvene shortly. You all have substantial matters to weigh."

"Right," Daren mutters, already halfway to the archway. "Later, then. Let me catch him before he puts a fist through a bulkhead."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Intro2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Intro2
TEXT:

"Wait for us!" Flower calls out, slipping off her seat and darting toward the open doorway to trail Daren. "We're coming with you!"

"Actually, I would request a private word with my nephew and Kate, if you would indulge me," Meridith says smoothly. "It will take only a moment."

Illuna and Melindra halt on the threshold. Their expressions harden in unison, sharp eyes sweeping between Meridith and Diane. Neither looks remotely comfortable leaving Barry and Kate behind in a subterranean bunker carved from solid seredium.

"It's fine," Kate tells them in a low, level tone, touching Melindra's arm. "Go on ahead. We'll catch up before Hadrik finds trouble."

"If you're certain," Melindra says, her jaw tight before giving a brief nod. "Come on, Flower. Let's make sure the dwarf doesn't start a riot."

The sound of their footsteps recedes down the corridor until the heavy stillness of the vault settles over the room once more. Meridith waits for the silence to deepen before turning her gaze upon us.

"I have a question for the two of you," Meridith says, leaning forward slightly. "Where does your fellowship truly stand regarding Eiden? Do your companions regard him as an ally or an enemy?"

"Isn't that something you should have settled before hiring us to assassinate him?" Kate asks, her dark eyes narrowing with razor-sharp skepticism.

"You are correct," Meridith concedes without hesitation. "I should have inquired sooner. I simply never envisioned that any rational traveler could take that monster's part—setting aside Melindra, given their personal history. But your dwarf companion's reaction forces a reassessment. Beyond his talk of warrior pride, Hadrik seemed genuinely loath to see Eiden killed. Enlighten me. Why?"

choice(""Eiden has helped us on several occasions, so it's hard to consider him an enemy."", B3-Ch11a-Suspicious, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Suspicious, v_b3_ch11_friend = 0)
choice(""Eiden has acted friendly with us in the past, but I've never trusted him."", B3-Ch11a-Suspicious, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Suspicious, v_b3_ch11_friend = 1)
choice(""I'd say that Eiden is neither our friend nor our foe, at the moment."", B3-Ch11a-Suspicious, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Suspicious, v_b3_ch11_friend = 2)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Suspicious
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch11_friend == 0) {
"Eiden has helped us on several occasions, so it's hard to consider him an enemy."

"Is that why he entrusted you with a message for me?" Meridith asks, studying my face. "Because he views your party as allies?"

"I doubt Eiden views anyone alive as an ally," Kate cuts in, her tone dry and cold. "The only reason we're still breathing is that we haven't threatened him. That calculus changes the exact second he realizes we signed on to help you butcher him."

"There is no reason Eiden should ever learn of our accord," Meridith replies smoothly. "We sit beneath tons of compressed seredium bedrock. Even his heightened senses cannot pierce these depths."

"You're forgetting that Eiden reads lies directly off human auras," Kate counters, folding her arms. "The moment we surface, if he suspects even a fraction of what happened down here, three pointed questions are all it will take to pull the whole scheme out of us."

"...A fair point," Meridith murmurs, her shoulders lowering as she exhales slowly. "Forgive me. In my urgency to eliminate him, I nearly pushed you into fatal exposure. Perhaps an adjusted arrangement is in order—one that even your principled dwarf might accept."

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_friend == 1) {
"Eiden has acted friendly with us in the past, but I've never trusted him."

"You distrust him, but what of the others?" Meridith asks. "Do they share your assessment?"

"None of us is foolish enough to trust Eiden," Kate says, her voice steady and sharp. "He toys with people because he's bored. But picking a fight with him while he leaves us alone is sheer suicide. If he turns his full power on us, we're dead in seconds."

"There is no need for open provocation," Meridith says. "Guard our secret, and you may treat with him exactly as you have before."

"You're forgetting that Eiden reads lies directly off human auras," Kate counters, folding her arms. "The moment we surface, if he suspects even a fraction of what happened down here, three pointed questions are all it will take to pull the whole scheme out of us."

"...A fair point," Meridith murmurs, her shoulders lowering as she exhales slowly. "Forgive me. In my urgency to eliminate him, I nearly pushed you into fatal exposure. Perhaps an adjusted arrangement is in order—one that even your principled dwarf might accept."

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_friend == 2) {
"I'd say that Eiden is neither our friend nor our foe, at the moment."

"I see," Meridith says, tapping her chin with one manicured finger. "And does the rest of your party agree?"

"I agree with Barry," Kate says, her gaze unwavering. "Eiden hasn't tried to kill us lately, but none of us has forgotten that he nearly incinerated us on a whim back in the woods. That said, seeking a fight with him is madness. If he decides to unleash his full power, there won't be enough left of us to bury."

"There is no need for open provocation," Meridith says. "Guard our secret, and you may treat with him exactly as you have before."

"You're forgetting that Eiden reads lies directly off human auras," Kate counters, folding her arms. "The moment we surface, if he suspects even a fraction of what happened down here, three pointed questions are all it will take to pull the whole scheme out of us."

"...A fair point," Meridith murmurs, her shoulders lowering as she exhales slowly. "Forgive me. In my urgency to eliminate him, I nearly pushed you into fatal exposure. Perhaps an adjusted arrangement is in order—one that even your principled dwarf might accept."

}
"What kind of deal?" I ask.

"Continue your participation in the tournament," Meridith says, leaning in. "That ensures your party remains on Varathia when our strike lands. Keep clearing regional objectives so the kings cannot disqualify you, and I guarantee your access to our Magium node—assuming our strike eliminates Eiden without your intervention. If he survives, the choice is entirely yours. Finish him, and the node is yours; refuse, and you walk away with nothing. But I will not demand your answer until that moment arrives. If Eiden confronts you on the surface, your auras will remain completely clean."

"Are you certain you want to offer that?" Kate asks, raising a brow. "You're shouldering all the risk."

"Granting access to the node costs me very little," Meridith says quietly. "Failing to kill Eiden costs me our entire civilization. If this revised bargain improves our odds by even the slimmest margin, it is worth every concession."

"The others will likely accept," Kate says. "Most of them joined the tournament for their own reasons anyway."

"I hope you are right," Meridith says.

"On another note," I say, stepping forward and tapping the bronze clasp at my waist. "You mentioned earlier that you possess the override ciphers to unlock the magical stats on my booster. Could you—"

"Of course," Meridith says, extending an open palm. "Pass it here. Though I must caution you: those two stats operate on principles fundamentally different from the twelve you are accustomed to."

"Differently how?" I ask.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Augmenting, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Augmenting)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Augmenting
TEXT:

"Magical Power and Magical Knowledge were the original two stats engineered into the early prototypes," Meridith explains, her fingers resting lightly on the metal casing. "The lessathi scholars who designed them sought to grant our people the ability to cast spells like natural mages. After decades of fruitless trials, they uncovered the bitter truth: pure lessathi lack the spiritual conduits required to channel spells directly from the magical plane. Only half-lessathi could ever ignite that link. Finding the stats useless to our own race, the elders locked them behind an encrypted cipher and developed twelve alternative stats governed by entirely different mechanisms."

"Different how?" I ask. "The device still siphons ambient energy from surrounding mages and converts it into stat points, doesn't it?"

"Your premise is incomplete," Meridith says. "Physical stats amplify what your mortal frame already possesses—muscle density, stride velocity, auditory acuity, synaptic reflexes—by replicating generalist enhancement spells through external resonance. But informational stats like ancient languages and combat technique do not alter your flesh at all. They store vast libraries of codified data within the booster's aura matrix. When you invest points, the mechanism establishes a calibrated interface with your mind, feeding indexed knowledge directly into your consciousness without frying your neural pathways. Sever the connection, and the knowledge evaporates instantly."

"Right," I say, nodding slowly as the mechanics click into place. "So the booster acts as an external catalyst. Physical stats mimic bodily enhancement spells, aura stats copy standard manipulation routines, and informational stats stream indexed archives straight through the aura tether."

"Precisely," Meridith says. "When our ancestors accepted that the device could never transform them into mages, they turned it into a machine that casts magic on their behalf."

"What about premonition?" Kate asks, leaning against the table. "No human spell foresees the future with that kind of certainty."

"That was extracted from a Magium node," Meridith says. "Ancient lessathi were far more adept at drawing fundamental secrets from the nodes than our scholars are today. Time weavers dispatched by the Magium perceive glimpses of diverging timelines; the stat booster mimics that phenomenon on a localized scale."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Implemented, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Implemented)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Implemented
TEXT:

"What about the two magic stats, then?" I ask. "Why are they so different from the rest?"

"They draw upon the same harvested energy reserve, but they bypass the manual allocation system entirely," Meridith explains. "When the prototypes contained only two stats, there was no point investment interface. The hardware fed incoming energy directly into those pathways as it accumulated. The point allocation matrix was developed much later for the twelve new stats, and by that era, research into the magic stats had been abandoned. You cannot assign points to them by hand. Once I break the cipher, they will draw from the device's internal reserves and level automatically as energy gathers."

"Wait," I say, catching the implication. "Will all incoming energy get sucked into magic? What happens to my point pool? Do I lose what I've already banked?"

"Your banked points remain untouched," Meridith reassures me. "Once energy has been processed into assignable points, the magic stats cannot convert it backward. You will still receive points for your standard stats over time, though the allocation rate will slow while the magical stats absorb their share."

"It can't be worse than Ollendor," I say with a faint grimace. "I didn't gather a single point the entire time we were inside the city."

"Our stationary extractors ringed the major settlements, where we anticipated tournament contestants would gather in force," Meridith says. "Once the kings refused to prohibit contestants from slaughtering one another in the wilderness, we dispatched mobile extraction squads to sweep the outer forests. Diane was commanding one of those units when you first crossed paths with her."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Inefficient, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Inefficient)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Inefficient
TEXT:

"So as long as I stay clear of cities and your extraction teams, points will trickle in," I say, unhooking the stat booster from my belt and passing it across the stone. "Here. Do you need any special tools to unlock them, or—"

"The device itself is sufficient," Meridith says. Her thumbs move across the recessed keypad in a swift, practiced cadence, entering a long string of cipher runes without glancing down. "Done. The magical pathways are unlocked."

"That's it?" I take the cool metal back, staring at the darkened screen. "I don't feel any different."

"The device is currently converting its existing energy reserves into stat levels," Meridith says. "When the first tier stabilizes, it will chime to alert you."

"Won't casting spells drain the device's energy?" Kate asks, eyeing the booster. "How will there be anything left to raise his physical stats?"

"The harvested energy pays only for the stat calibrations," Meridith clarifies. "The stats themselves grant Barry the physiological capacity to channel energy from the magical plane directly, exactly like a born caster. That is why pure lessathi could never operate them. Our auras contain no natural magical conduit, making direct casting impossible regardless of mechanical ingenuity."

"If I can tap the magical plane myself," I say, looking up, "can I feed energy into the booster manually instead of waiting on passive extraction? There are tools for channeling mana into enchanted items. I've seen enchanters do it."

"Pouring raw energy into the booster is crude and inefficient," Meridith says, shaking her head. "Without proper aura extraction filtration, the charge bleeds off in minutes. You should already understand that from Emergency Mode. In fact... I never inquired how you managed to trigger that failsafe in the first place. Spontaneous activation is extraordinarily rare. Did you discover a manual override?"

choice(""I got the option on the screen while I was fighting some skeleton stillwaters."", B3-Ch11a-Ridiculous, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Ridiculous, v_b3_ch11_transferred = 0)
choice(""The first time I used it, Eiden transferred his energy into the device to activate it."", B3-Ch11a-Ridiculous, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Ridiculous, v_b3_ch11_transferred = 1)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Ridiculous
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch11_transferred == 0) {
"I got the option on the screen while I was fighting some skeleton stillwaters."

"Ah," Meridith nods thoughtfully. "Their reserves were overflowing far beyond what their damaged auras could contain. The atmospheric leakage tripped the emergency threshold."

"Exactly," I say.

"Is there any way to trigger Emergency Mode on command?" Kate asks. "If several of us poured our mana into the booster at once, would that force the threshold?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_transferred == 1) {
"The first time I used it, Eiden transferred his energy into the device to activate it."

"Eiden?" Meridith's brow arches in genuine surprise. "Why would he do that? Whom were you fighting?"

"Tyrath," I say. "The dragon king had us pinned down in the ruins. Eiden stopped time and forced power into my device to balance the odds."

"Of course," Meridith murmurs. "He had everything to gain from seeing an ancient rival crippled without getting his own hands dirty. If what you say is accurate, Eiden's aura imprint may still linger in the hardware matrix. Hand it back to me for a moment."

I pass the booster across the table. Meridith draws a slender wand of polished seredium and touches its tip to the casing. A pulse of vivid cerulean light ripples across the metal, humming with sharp resonance for three slow heartbeats before sinking into the circuitry.

"Just as I suspected," Meridith says, her expression sharpening. "The device registered Eiden's unique aura signature as a compatible emergency reservoir. Whenever he is within close proximity, pressing this auxiliary stud three times in rapid succession will forcibly tap into his ambient reserve."

"Can I drain him dry?" I ask, taking the booster back into my palm.

"Hardly," Meridith says with a dry scoff. "The moment he detects the siphon, Eiden will sever the link in a heartbeat, and you will never catch him unprepared again. But it will purchase a split-second surge—enough for one or two decisive spells. In a confrontation against him, a fraction of a second is the difference between survival and obliteration."

"Good to know," I say, turning the device over. "A last resort."

"Is there any way to trigger Emergency Mode on command?" Kate asks. "If several of us poured our mana into the booster at once, would that force the threshold?"

}
"The energy required is astronomical," Meridith explains, shaking her head. "Only a stillwater possesses that volume of uninhibited raw power, and Melindra lacks the precise discipline to channel it without tearing the device apart. The rest of you would exhaust yourselves into unconsciousness for nothing."

"Understood," Kate says quietly.

"Diane, you have remained remarkably silent since returning from the surface," Meridith says, shifting her focus to her second-in-command. "Is something troubling you?"

"No, Lady Meridith," Diane answers immediately, keeping her posture formal and hands clasped. "I recognized the gravity of this negotiation and did not wish to interrupt your council."

"Speak freely when you have thoughts," Meridith tells her gently. "I have always valued your perspective."

"Thank you, Lady Meridith," Diane says with a respectful bow of her head. "I will speak up if there is anything to add."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Insane, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Insane)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Insane
TEXT:

"Very well," Meridith says, rising smoothly from the stone slab. "Our business here is concluded for the moment. We should locate Daren and the others before they stumble into restricted sectors."

We rise and follow her out into the crystal-lit corridor. As we walk past the heavy, concealed doorway leading to the Magium node chamber, a question that has been nagging at me surfaces.

"You mentioned giving us access to the node," I say, keeping pace beside her. "How does that actually function in practice? How do your researchers interface with it?"

"Our scholars spent centuries engineering specialized buffer instruments to extract data safely," Meridith explains, her heels clicking rhythmically against the stone. "It is slow, exacting work, but immensely fruitful. The wand I carry was crafted entirely from schematics retrieved from that very node, as were many of our finest relics."

"Are mechanical tools the only path?" Kate asks. "No direct interface?"

"Direct connection is possible, but perilous in the extreme," Meridith says, her voice lowering. "You can tether your aura straight into the node to project your consciousness into the Magium realm. The breadth of access is infinitely wider, but a single misstep or stray thought will trap your mind on that plane forever. To an observer in the physical world, your body simply stands frozen with glowing yellow eyes until you starve to death on your feet."

Direct aura tethering. Exactly what Arraka had done during the God of Time's trial.

"What happens if someone touches the physical sphere itself?" Kate asks.

"Catastrophe," Meridith says, her expression darkening with grim memory. "Every few years, an unauthorized lessathi slips past security to lay bare hands upon it. Almost without exception, they lose their sanity in the first instant. They cease speaking, refuse all nourishment, and turn violently feral if anyone attempts to drag them away. They sit staring into the light until dehydration claims them."

"You left us alone in that chamber knowing that?" Kate's voice turns dangerously cold, her dark eyes flashing. "Without a single word of warning?"

"I saw no necessity for one," Meridith replies, a defensive edge creeping into her tone. "Pure lessathi feel an innate, subconscious pull toward the node, requiring strict quarantine protocols. But other races exhibit no such compulsion—not even half-lessathi. Why would any sane creature touch a lethal anomaly without an instinct compelling them?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Faction, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Faction)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Faction
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch10_node == 1) {
"Ask Barry," Kate says dryly, casting a sharp side-glance at me. "He touched it before we ever set foot in your conference room."

"He touched the node?" Meridith halts mid-stride, turning to stare at me in stark disbelief. "How are you still standing here, speaking coherent sentences? Why hasn't your mind dissolved?"

"I only made contact for a fraction of a second," I say, rubbing the back of my neck. "The deluge of raw data felt like an ocean slamming into the inside of my skull. I yanked my hand back before it cracked my sanity."

"You resisted the seductive pull of infinite knowledge in under a second?" Meridith shakes her head slowly, looking at me as if I were a newly discovered species. "If you possess that caliber of mental discipline, why do something so monumentally reckless in the first place? The more I observe you, the less sense you make."

"Don't waste your breath analyzing it," Kate says, shaking her head. "Barry routinely throws himself into lethal situations where a heartbeat determines survival. Statistically, simple luck cannot account for the fact that he's still breathing. He simply has a bizarre talent for making the correct gamble under impossible pressure—except when he doesn't, and blind fortune bails him out."

"I see," Meridith says slowly, her gaze lingering on me a moment longer.

"Do we know which way the others went?" Diane asks, scanning the shadowed junction ahead. "The seredium walls dampen aura tracking completely."

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_node == 0) {
"Ask Barry," Kate says dryly, casting a sharp side-glance at me. "He came within an inch of touching it before we walked into your office."

"You did?" Meridith turns on me, her eyes widening in disbelief. "Why would you even consider such madness? Any creature without our racial compulsion should recoil on pure instinct!"

"Barry's survival instincts are fundamentally defective," Kate says, not missing a beat. "Do you honestly think a sane man enters a death tournament against elite continent-class mages without knowing how to cast a single spell?"

"I suppose that is hard to dispute," Meridith murmurs.

"I pulled back in the end," I point out. "Even I have boundaries."

"Do we know which way the others went?" Diane asks, scanning the shadowed junction ahead. "The seredium walls dampen aura tracking completely."

}
"We passed our original entrance tunnel," Kate says. "Hadrik couldn't open that sealed door without leveling the wall, so he kept walking."

"There are only two exits from this junction," Meridith says. "One leads deeper into our territory; the other cuts straight into Heksol faction ground. We should check the Heksol corridor first."

"Isn't that tunnel marked in the common tongue?" Diane asks. "Surely he read the signs and took the other path."

choice(""Yeah, if it's marked like you say, then he probably avoided that tunnel."", B3-Ch11a-Avoided, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Avoided, v_b3_ch11_path = 0)
choice(""Actually, he might have had even more of a reason to go there, if it's marked."", B3-Ch11a-Reason, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Reason, v_b3_ch11_path = 1)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Avoided
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch11_hear,1) if (v_hearing >= 2)
set(v_b3_ch11_hear,0) if (v_hearing < 2)
#if(v_hearing >= 2) {
"Yeah, if it's marked like you say, then he probably avoided that tunnel."

"We will check the main corridor first, then," Meridith says. "I only hope he hasn't provoked an incident."

"It's Hadrik," I say. "If he ran into someone from Heksol's faction, he probably poured them a drink before they could draw steel."

"I hope your confidence is warranted," Meridith says.

As we near the fork, Hadrik's booming laugh drifts down the passage, rich and unmistakable.

"Hahaha! You know, for a Heksol guard, you're all right. Here, try this vintage!"

"I still have my eye on you, dwarf," a second voice answers, thick with suspicion. "Don't think a free glass makes us brothers—wait. This is different. What's in this bottle?"

"A discerning palate!" Hadrik roars. "Subtle, but distinct. Tell you what: three bottles, three vintages. Rank them by price after one sip, and you keep the lot. What do you say?"

"Bring them out," the guard says. "If there's one thing I know, it's alcohol."

"Ha! That's the spirit!"

I glance at the others. Neither Meridith nor Kate reacts; the dense seredium walls muffle the sound for ordinary ears, but my hearing stat caught every word clearly. He's definitely down in Heksol territory.

}
#if(v_hearing < 2) {
"Yeah, if it's marked like you say, then he probably avoided that tunnel."

"We will check the main corridor first, then," Meridith says. "I only hope he hasn't provoked an incident."

"It's Hadrik," I say. "If he ran into someone from Heksol's faction, he probably poured them a drink before they could draw steel."

"I hope your confidence is warranted," Meridith says.

We take the left fork, following the carved markers toward the main lessathi residential quarter.

Around the second bend, we find Daren, Flower, and Melindra squared off against a lessathi checkpoint guard in gray tunics.

"Listen to me," Daren says, his voice rising in strained exasperation. "I don't have time for bureaucratic riddles. Did a dwarf come through here or not?"

"I have already stated that a dwarf may or may not have passed this post," the guard replies evenly, resting a hand on his ledger. "If such an individual entered, his documentation was in order. Which brings us back to yours. Present your clearance papers, or I will escort the lot of you to holding cells."

"We are Lady Meridith's guests!" Daren snaps. "How do you think we arrived? Walking through the bedrock?"

"Lady Meridith is not the only official authorized to operate the transit gate," the guard says. "Unauthorized surface entries are handled by standing protocol."

"Then ask her yourself," Daren points behind him. "She's right here."

The guard stiffens and bows low. "Lady Meridith! I was unaware of your presence today. I was detaining these trespassers who claim—"

"I heard," Meridith cuts him off with an icy wave. "They are with me, as is the dwarf. Did he pass through here?"

"No dwarf has crossed this post today, Lady Meridith," the guard admits. "Shall I organize a search party?"

Meridith sighs. "No. He took the Heksol tunnel. Release these humans so we can retrieve him."

"Of course," the guard says, stepping aside. "Though in the future, proper advance notification would prevent misunderstandings..."

"I will ensure the paperwork is filed," Meridith says dryly. "Let us move."

As Daren and Melindra rejoin us, Flower narrows her eyes and sticks her tongue out at the guard before turning on her heel.

}
choice("Tell the others where Hadrik went.", B3-Ch11a-Went, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Went, v_b3_ch11_reverse = 1) if (v_hearing >= 2)
choice("Remain silent, and keep going towards the wrong tunnel.", B3-Ch11a-Silent, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Silent, v_b3_ch11_reverse = 0) if (v_hearing >= 2)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Observation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Observation) if (v_perception >= 4 && v_hearing < 2)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Behind, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Behind) if (v_hearing < 2 && v_perception < 4)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Silent
TEXT:

I keep quiet. Hadrik sounded like he had the situation well under control, and interrupting a dwarf midway through an ale contest never ends well.

We follow the left fork toward the primary lessathi sector.

Around the second bend, we find Daren, Flower, and Melindra squared off against a lessathi checkpoint guard.

"Listen to me," Daren says, his voice rising in exasperation. "I don't have time for bureaucratic riddles. Did a dwarf come through here or not?"

"I have already stated that a dwarf may or may not have passed this post," the guard replies evenly. "If such an individual entered, his documentation was in order. Which brings us back to yours. Present your clearance papers, or I will escort the lot of you to holding cells."

"We are Lady Meridith's guests!" Daren snaps. "How do you think we arrived? Walking through the bedrock?"

"Lady Meridith is not the only official authorized to operate the transit gate," the guard says. "Unauthorized surface entries are handled by standing orders."

"Then ask her yourself," Daren points behind him. "She's right here."

The guard stiffens and bows low. "Lady Meridith! I was unaware of your presence today. I was detaining these trespassers who claim—"

"I heard," Meridith cuts him off. "They are with me, as is the dwarf. Did he pass through here?"

"No dwarf has crossed this post today, Lady Meridith," the guard admits. "Shall I organize a search party?"

Meridith sighs. "No. He took the Heksol tunnel. Release these humans so we can retrieve him."

"Of course," the guard says, stepping aside. "Though in the future, proper advance notification would prevent misunderstandings..."

"I will ensure the paperwork is filed," Meridith says dryly. "Let us move."

As Daren and Melindra rejoin us, Flower narrows her eyes and sticks her tongue out at the guard before turning on her heel.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Observation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Observation) if (v_perception >= 4)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Behind, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Behind) if (v_perception < 4)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Observation
TEXT:

As we turn to leave, a section of the seredium masonry catches my eye. The seam doesn't match the surrounding bedrock.

"Barry?" Daren pauses, looking back. "What is it?"

"This stonework," I say, running my fingers over the edge. The texture is smoother, cut with unnatural precision. "This tunnel was engineered by lessathi hands, wasn't it?"

"These shafts predate living memory," Meridith says. "Why does that matter?"

"An ancient lessathi architect left puzzle pieces hidden across every site he constructed," I say. "Given that this is the primary lessathi redoubt, this wall looks suspicious. Melindra, Flower—take a look."

"We do not have time for games," Meridith protests. "Your companion could be in the middle of a brawl with Heksol guards—"

"Hadrik is fine," I assure her.

Melindra steps up and taps the stone with a knuckle. "The masonry is artificial, but I don't feel a mechanical latch. Flower?"

"I know a few old ruin knock codes," Flower says brightly. "Let me try!"

"This is absurd," Meridith mutters. "It will take hours."

"Watch," Flower says. She raps a rhythmic sequence against the seam, pauses, then knocks again. "Two down. Three left!"

On the third sequence, the stone slab grinds backward into the wall and slides aside. A gray rectangular block emerges in its place, engraved in the common tongue: 'If you like discovering secrets, place your palm upon this rock.'

Flower gasps, reading the script. "Barry, you were right! It's the architect!"

She presses her palm against the stone. With a soft click, a metal puzzle token ejects from a narrow slit beneath the inscription, followed by a sliver of parchment reading: 'Find all of the pieces of the puzzle, and I will share with you my greatest secret. I've left one of these in each of the buildings I've designed.'

"Barry, hurry!" Flower urges. "Put your hand on it before it resets!"

choice("Place my palm on the stone.", B3-Ch11a-Heksol, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Heksol, v_b3_ch11_puzzle4 = 1)
choice("Refuse to take a puzzle piece for myself.", B3-Ch11a-Heksol, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Heksol, v_b3_ch11_puzzle4 = 0)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Heksol
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch11_fourth,1) if (v_b3_ch11_puzzle4 == 1)
achievement("Fourth piece of the puzzle",v_ac_b3_ch11_fourth)
#if(v_b3_ch11_puzzle4 == 1) {
I place my palm flat against the carved surface. A matching puzzle piece drops into my hand with a duplicate parchment. The mechanism clicks, and the gray block withdraws, sealing the seredium wall flush once more.

"Satisfied?" Meridith asks, checking the corridor. "Can we proceed?"

"All done," I say, pocketing the piece. "Lead the way."

"Let us hope Hadrik was halted at the perimeter," Meridith says.

We backtrack to the junction and head down the right tunnel into Heksol territory.

Minutes later, Hadrik's voice carries around the bend.

"Sounds like your friend and I would have seen eye to eye," Hadrik is saying. "Pity I missed him."

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_puzzle4 == 0) {
"I don't need one," I say. "I only pointed it out because you're collecting them."

"Really?" Flower's eyes widen. "Thanks, Barry! I owe you!"

"Don't worry about it," I say as the mechanism resets, sliding the stone flush with the wall.

"Satisfied?" Meridith asks, checking the corridor. "Can we proceed?"

"All done," I say. "Lead the way."

"Let us hope Hadrik was halted at the perimeter," Meridith says.

We backtrack to the junction and head down the right tunnel into Heksol territory.

Minutes later, Hadrik's voice carries around the bend.

"Sounds like your friend and I would have seen eye to eye," Hadrik is saying. "Pity I missed him."

}
"He died a proper dwarf death," the guard replies. "Picked a drunken brawl with the wrong crew and took a blade through the ribs. Dragged four of them down with him before he dropped. Always said a tavern fight was the only way to go, so he got his wish."

We round the corner. Hadrik sits on an upturned crate, surrounded by empty dwarven ale bottles, raising a fresh glass with a grinning lessathi guard.

"Well, look who decided to join the party!" Hadrik calls out, waving a bottle. "The high-and-mighty boss herself! Don't just stand there, Meridith—pull up a rock and have a drink!"

"I must decline," Meridith says stiffly. "I have critical meetings that require a clear head."

"Is she always this stiff?" Hadrik asks the guard, refilling both cups.

"Every waking minute," the guard replies, taking a deep draught.

"Hadrik, damn it," Daren groans. "Do you know what we went through looking for you? The checkpoint guard down the other corridor nearly threw us in a cell!"

"Not my fault you look like bandits," Hadrik laughs. "Look at me. I ran into a guard and we're getting along great."

"Don't give me ideas, dwarf," the guard chuckles, clinking his mug against Hadrik's.

"Spare me the posturing, Kleykarth," Meridith says cold. "I came to ensure my guest hadn't started a riot in your sector. Clearly, my concern was misplaced."

"The dwarf had far more grievance with you than with us, 'Lady' Meridith," Kleykarth sneers. "He shared some choice thoughts about your leadership over the first round. Care to hear them?"

"I would be fascinated," Meridith says, fixing Hadrik with a sharp gaze.

"No need, no need!" Hadrik claps a heavy hand on the guard's shoulder.

"Hahaha!" Kleykarth barks a laugh. "Just pulling your beard, dwarf. Your secrets are safe with me."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Uverg, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Uverg)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Behind
TEXT:

We leave the checkpoint behind, backtrack to the junction, and turn down the right corridor into Heksol territory.

Minutes later, Hadrik's voice carries around the bend.

"Sounds like your friend and I would have seen eye to eye," Hadrik is saying. "Pity I missed him."

"He died a proper dwarf death," the guard replies. "Picked a drunken brawl with the wrong crew and took a blade through the ribs. Dragged four of them down with him before he dropped. Always said a tavern fight was the only way to go, so he got his wish."

We round the corner. Hadrik sits on an upturned wooden crate, surrounded by three empty dwarven ale bottles, raising a foaming mug with a grinning lessathi guard in dark leathers.

"Well, look who decided to join the party!" Hadrik calls out, waving a bottle in greeting. "The high-and-mighty boss herself! Don't just stand there, Meridith—pull up a rock and have a drink!"

"I must decline," Meridith says stiffly, her eyes narrowing. "I have critical meetings that require a clear head."

"Is she always this stiff?" Hadrik asks the guard, refilling both cups from a fresh flask.

"Every waking minute," the guard replies, taking a deep draught and wiping his mouth on his sleeve.

"Hadrik, damn it," Daren groans, shaking his head. "Do you know what we went through looking for you? The checkpoint guard down the other corridor nearly threw us in a cell!"

"Not my fault you look like bandits," Hadrik laughs, slapping his knee. "Look at me. I ran into a sentry and we're getting along famously."

"Don't give me ideas, dwarf," the guard chuckles, clinking his mug against Hadrik's.

"Spare me the posturing, Kleykarth," Meridith says in a low, cold tone. "I came to ensure my guest hadn't started a riot in your sector. Clearly, my concern was misplaced."

"The dwarf had far more grievance with you than with us, 'Lady' Meridith," Kleykarth sneers. "He shared some choice thoughts about your leadership over the first round. Care to hear them?"

"I would be fascinated," Meridith says, fixing Hadrik with an unyielding glare.

"No need, no need!" Hadrik claps a heavy hand on the guard's shoulder, laughing nervously.

"Hahaha!" Kleykarth barks a laugh. "Just pulling your beard, dwarf. Your secrets are safe with me."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Uverg, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Uverg)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Reason
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch11_hear,1) if (v_hearing >= 2)
set(v_b3_ch11_hear,0) if (v_hearing < 2)
#if(v_hearing >= 2) {
"Actually, he might have had even more of a reason to go there, if it's marked."

"Why?" Diane asks, glancing at me.

"He didn't leave your office fond of Meridith," I say. "He probably jumped at the chance to share a drink with people who despise her just as much."

"A reasonable deduction," Meridith says dryly. "We check the Heksol tunnel first. Follow me."

As we approach the fork, Hadrik's booming laugh carries down the stone.

"Hahaha! You know, for a Heksol guard, you're all right. Here, try this one!"

"I still have my eye on you, dwarf," a second voice answers, thick with suspicion. "Don't think a free glass makes us—wait. This is different. What's in this bottle?"

"A discerning palate!" Hadrik roars. "Subtle, but distinct. Tell you what: three bottles, three vintages. Rank them by price after one sip, and you keep the lot. What do you say?"

"Bring them out," the guard says. "If there's one thing I know, it's alcohol."

"Ha! That's the spirit!"

No one else reacts; the seredium walls muffle the sound for ordinary ears, but my hearing stat caught every word. He is definitely in Heksol territory.

We take the right fork, moving deeper into rival ground.

Minutes later, Hadrik's voice rings out again.

"Beaten fair and square!" Hadrik bellows. "Take the bottles—you earned them!"

}
#if(v_hearing < 2) {
"Actually, he might have had even more of a reason to go there, if it's marked."

"Why?" Diane asks, glancing at me.

"He didn't leave your office fond of Meridith," I say. "He probably jumped at the chance to share a drink with people who despise her just as much."

"A reasonable deduction," Meridith says dryly. "We check the Heksol tunnel first. Follow me."

We take the right fork at the junction, heading into Heksol territory.

Minutes later, Hadrik's booming voice carries around the corner.

"Beaten fair and square!" Hadrik bellows. "Take the bottles—you earned them!"

}
"Damn right I did," the guard shoots back. "Did you think two identical vintages and a third with a hair's breadth of difference would throw me off? My old dwarf drinking partner was ten times the soak you are. He'd have put you under the table in three rounds."

"I don't doubt it," Hadrik says, raising his glass high as we round the bend. "A toast to him, then. May he look down on us with a full mug and a drunken grin!"

"Dwarf," the guard says, "that might be the first respectable thing you've said all day."

They clink glasses and drink deep before spotting us.

"Well, look who decided to show up!" Hadrik grins broadly. "The high-and-mighty boss herself! Pull up a crate, Meridith. Have a drink!"

"I must decline," Meridith says stiffly. "I have urgent matters requiring a sober mind."

"Is she always this stiff?" Hadrik asks, refilling the guard's glass.

"Every waking minute," the guard replies.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Fellow, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Fellow)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Went
TEXT:

"Actually, Hadrik went down the Heksol tunnel," I say. "I just overheard him talking to a guard."

"I knew it," Meridith says, picking up her pace. "We need to intervene before he gives them another reason to hate us."

"Sounded more like a drinking contest than a fight," I add.

"Either way, we cannot leave them unsupervised," Meridith says. "Come."

We take the right corridor at the junction, moving quickly through the carved passage.

Minutes later, Hadrik's booming voice carries around the bend.

"Beaten fair and square!" Hadrik bellows. "Take the bottles—you earned them!"

"Damn right I did," the guard shoots back. "Did you think two identical vintages and a third with a hair's breadth of difference would throw me off? My old dwarf drinking partner was ten times the soak you are. He'd have put you under the table in three rounds."

"I don't doubt it," Hadrik says, raising his glass high as we round the bend. "A toast to him, then. May he look down on us with a full mug and a drunken grin!"

"Dwarf," the guard says, "that might be the first respectable thing you've said all day."

They clink glasses and drink deep before spotting us.

"Well, look who decided to show up!" Hadrik grins broadly. "The high-and-mighty boss herself! Pull up a crate, Meridith. Have a drink!"

"I must decline," Meridith says stiffly. "I have urgent matters requiring a sober mind."

"Is she always this stiff?" Hadrik asks, refilling the guard's glass.

"Every waking minute," the guard replies.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Fellow, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Fellow)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Fellow
TEXT:

"What were you two doing?" Kate asks, looking at the row of bottles.

"A tasting wager," Hadrik says cheerfully. "Ranked three bottles by cost on a single sip and took the prize. Care for a round, Kate? I have more in the pack—"

"No," Kate says flatly. "Just curious."

"What brings you to our sector, 'Lady' Meridith?" Kleykarth asks, setting his mug down with deliberate insolence. "It has been a while since we were blessed with your presence."

"Spare me the posturing, Kleykarth," Meridith says. "I came to ensure my guest had not started a riot among your ranks. Clearly, my concern was unwarranted."

"The dwarf had far more grievance with you than with us," Kleykarth smirks. "He shared some choice thoughts about your leadership over the first round. Care to hear them?"

"I would be fascinated," Meridith says, fixing Hadrik with a cool stare.

"No need, no need!" Hadrik cuts in, clapping a heavy hand on Kleykarth's shoulder.

"Hahaha!" Kleykarth barks a laugh. "Just pulling your beard, dwarf. Your secrets are safe with me."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Uverg, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Uverg)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Uverg
TEXT:

"Phew," Hadrik mutters, taking another sip. "Thought you had me there."

"Did I hear correctly?" a voice calls from the shadows further down the shaft. "Insults so severe that even a drunken dwarf hesitates to repeat them? Now I am intrigued."

"Finally decided to step into the light, did you?" Hadrik pulls a third glass from his pack and begins pouring. "I've been yelling into the dark for ten minutes. Pull up a seat!"

"Standing suits me fine," the voice replies.

A man steps into the lantern light. A leather boot sits squarely on his head, wool socks cover both hands, and arcane scribbles cover his tunic and trousers. He walks barefoot on the seredium stone—not entirely shocking, given that Flower has traveled shoeless since we met.

"You want to hear the insults?" Kleykarth asks him. "You were standing fifty feet away. Surely you heard them."

"I was in a trance," the boot-wearing man replies earnestly. "I heard nothing of your conversation. My apologies, good sir."

"A trance?" Hadrik squints at him. "What are you, a shaman?"

"Nothing so grand," the man says. "I was preparing my thoughts to commune with our Creator. He speaks to me on occasion... though distinguishing his voice from my own delusions grows difficult of late."

"Don't pay him any mind," Kleykarth tells Hadrik. "He lost his mind touching the Magium node months ago. Talks in riddles all day."

"Not all my words are riddles," the man protests mildly. "And you are being remarkably rude to our guest. You speak so loudly the poor fellow hasn't found the courage to utter a syllable. Come now, friend—join us! These people may look rough, but they will not mock your appearance."

He is staring directly at a blue crystal cluster protruding from the ceiling.

"What is it, Uverg?" Kleykarth sneers. "Is your imaginary crystal fairy too shy to speak?"

"I doubt he is a fairy," Uverg replies innocently. "Fairies rarely transfer their consciousness into seredium minerals."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Protocol, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Protocol)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Protocol
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
Transferring consciousness into blue crystals?

"Friend, will you not give us a name?" Uverg asks, still gazing at the crystal. "Even for me, talking to myself grows tiresome."

"Query imprecise," a flat metallic voice rings from the faceted stone. "The Overseer protocol cannot generate an accurate response to ambiguous inputs. Rephrase."

"The Overseer protocol?" Meridith's head snaps up. "Where have I heard that name?"

"The construct that governed the aerial sentinels in Ollendor," I tell her. "We destroyed its chassis, but it uploaded its consciousness into the crystal network before it died."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
Transferring consciousness into blue crystals?

"Friend, will you not give us a name?" Uverg asks, still gazing at the crystal. "Even for me, talking to myself grows tiresome."

"Query imprecise," a flat metallic voice rings from the faceted stone. "The Overseer protocol cannot generate an accurate response to ambiguous inputs. Rephrase."

"The Overseer protocol?" Meridith's head snaps up. "Where have I heard that name?"

"The construct that governs the aerial sentinels in Ollendor," I tell her. "It uses the crystal matrix to spy across distances."

}
"Ah," Meridith says. "Reports reached us that the previous king reactivated an ancient lessathi automaton, but details were scarce. Maintaining informants in Ollendor was far too dangerous. How does a machine occupy mineral veins? Does it possess a soul?"

"Its creators manufactured a synthetic soul to grant it autonomy," Kate explains. "When the Magium seized control of its core, the unit retained fragments of higher knowledge after the link severed. That is how it navigates the network."

"It interfaced directly with the Creator?" Meridith murmurs. "Remarkable."

"You in the crystal," Diane calls upward. "How long have you been listening?"

"The network registered foreign signatures the moment your party teleported underground," the Overseer drones. "I have monitored your movements since."

"You overheard our conference?" Diane demands.

"Affirmative. Your designs concerning the stillwater known as Eiden are peripheral to my mandate. My primary objective is analyzing the mineral matrix and the Magium itself. Your subterranean node will accelerate my inquiries considerably."

"You expect to tap our node without authorization?" Meridith's voice drops to a menacing rasp.

"Correct," the Overseer replies. "The Overseer protocol deems your consent irrelevant, as you possess no viable mechanism to restrict my access. If you contest this assessment, submit an appeal to a green-eyed unit at your earliest convenience. Your feedback is valued."

Meridith turns to me, her jaw clenched tight. "Is this machine mocking me?"

choice(""Welcome to the club. He's been mocking us like this since the first day we met him."", B3-Ch11a-Exchange, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Exchange, v_b3_ch11_mocking = 0)
choice(""Actually, I think he just can't stop himself from talking like this."", B3-Ch11a-Exchange, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Exchange, v_b3_ch11_mocking = 1)
choice(""I've always found it difficult to tell if he's being serious or not in these situations."", B3-Ch11a-Exchange, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Exchange, v_b3_ch11_mocking = 2)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Exchange
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch11_mocking == 0) {
"Welcome to the club. He's been mocking us like this since the first day we met him."

"It possesses an infuriating talent for provocation," Meridith says through gritted teeth. "I am sorely tempted to fetch a ladder and shatter the stone."

"I wouldn't," Hadrik warns. "The last man who tried smashing one of those didn't walk away."

"A figure of speech," Meridith says coldly. "I am well aware of the hazards."

"Pardon me, Overseer," Uverg interrupts, leaning forward. "Did you state that you made direct contact with our Creator?"

"If by 'Creator' you designate the Magium, affirmative," the Overseer responds.

"Incredible!" Uverg beams. "A fellow traveler of the higher realm! Would you consider an exchange of theoretical findings? We have much to gain from mutual cooperation."

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_mocking == 1) {
"Actually, I think he just can't stop himself from talking like this."

"You claim it has no control over what it says?" Meridith's eyes narrow. "Hardly. It calculates every syllable. It is deliberately inciting a response."

"Kate is right," I say. "It uses scripted courtesy specifically where it causes maximum irritation."

"Pardon me, Overseer," Uverg interrupts, leaning forward. "Did you state that you made direct contact with our Creator?"

"If by 'Creator' you designate the Magium, affirmative," the Overseer responds.

"Incredible!" Uverg beams. "A fellow traveler of the higher realm! Would you consider an exchange of theoretical findings? We have much to gain from mutual cooperation."

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_mocking == 2) {
"I've always found it difficult to tell if he's being serious or not in these situations."

"It isn't serious," Kate says. "It deploys institutional phrasing specifically to inflame the listener. It finds amusement in it."

"So it seems," Meridith says darkly. "I assumed machines were sterile calculators. This one clearly understands malice."

"Pardon me, Overseer," Uverg interrupts, leaning forward. "Did you state that you made direct contact with our Creator?"

"If by 'Creator' you designate the Magium, affirmative," the Overseer responds.

"Incredible!" Uverg beams. "A fellow traveler of the higher realm! Would you consider an exchange of theoretical findings? We have much to gain from mutual cooperation."

}
"The Overseer protocol accepts your proposal," the voice chimes from the stone. "Your ability to isolate my signature within the matrix merits further study. We will schedule a private consultation."

"You will conduct no private discussions concerning the Magium on my authority," Meridith snaps.

"Processing..." the Overseer hums. "Hostility detected from lessathi representative Meridith. Diagnostic assessment indicates unwarranted aggression stemming from an underlying inferiority complex. Furthermore, behavioral patterns suggest displaced frustration arising from administrative incompetence and an inability to unify her faction."

Kleykarth erupts into deafening laughter. "Administrative incompetence! Priceless! I like this machine! Hold your meeting in Heksol territory, Overseer. We'll grant all the privacy you need, and her ladyship can't do a damn thing about it. Right, leader?"

"You test my patience, Kleykarth," Meridith says, the air around her dropping several degrees.

"What will you do?" Kleykarth sneers. "Butcher us like you butchered Heksol and have your tame acid-caster dissolve the bones? Speak plain, Meridith!"

"No," Meridith says, forcing her voice level. "I will not dignify baseless treason with a response."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Crafty, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Crafty)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Crafty
TEXT:

"Baseless?" Kleykarth takes another swallow of ale. "Every lessathi in these shafts knows what happened to Heksol. The man had his flaws, but he took power through legitimate contest, not back-alley slaughter. You will never hold our respect, Meridith. Live with it."

"Is that a threat?" Meridith asks quietly.

"A statement of terms," Kleykarth says. "We don't butcher our own blood for political advantage. We cooperate when our interests align, as with this tournament. But leave our sector alone, or things turn less civil. Take that however you like."

"I will confer with your faction commander," Meridith says. "I trust he retains some measure of sanity."

"I'll brief him myself," Kleykarth grins. "He'll welcome a fellow Meridith-hater with open arms. Offer stands for you too, dwarf. You'd fit right in."

"Appreciate the thought, friend," Hadrik says, "but I'm not staying long enough to unpack. Next time."

"I'll file your visitor clearance anyway," Kleykarth chuckles.

"The Overseer protocol requests an audience with your commander as well," the crystal chimes. "May I accompany your delegation?"

"Make yourself at home," Kleykarth says. "We treat our guests with respect, unlike her ladyship."

"Gratifying," the Overseer drones. "I anticipate a prosperous partnership."

"Is it just me," Kate whispers leaning in, "or did the machine pick a fight with Meridith specifically to win over the Heksol faction?"

"Calculated maneuvering," I whisper back.

"Was it always this cunning?" she asks.

"Always," I tell her. "It just stopped pretending to be polite."

"I have concurrent tasks requiring processing cycles," the Overseer announces. "I shall return when citizen Kleykarth convenes with his leadership. Good day."

"Wait!" Meridith commands. "Overseer!"

The crystal goes dull. The resonance fades.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Infiltrate, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Infiltrate)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Infiltrate
TEXT:

"Unbelievable," Meridith seethes. "The insolence."

"Stings when people ignore orders, doesn't it?" Kleykarth laughs.

"Your tongue wags faster than your wits," Meridith says, turning on him with absolute, freezing venom. "Push me once more, Kleykarth. See what remains of you."

The laughter dies in the guard's throat. His smirk crumbles into wary silence; even soaked in dwarven ale, the threat lands hard.

"Peace, everyone," Uverg intervenes smoothly. "The drink is talking, nothing more."

"You disappoint me most of all, Uverg," Meridith says, fixing him with a cold glare. "When the council demanded you be confined as a madman, I personally guaranteed your lucidity. Now you harbor a hostile entity in our stronghold? What happened to remaining neutral?"

"Lady Meridith, no betrayal was intended," Uverg says with a bow. "The Overseer will enter Heksol's halls whether I participate or not, and any lore he yields will remain locked behind their doors. But if I engage him in study, I will deliver my findings directly to you. Like it or not, the construct has infiltrated our redoubt. Better to extract value from his presence than force an enemy."

"Can we sever his connection to the veins?" Meridith asks.

"Not with our current understanding," Uverg admits. "Now that he knows I can isolate his frequency, he will mask his presence more thoroughly. Shutting down discourse gains us nothing."

Meridith pauses, considering. "A bitter pill, but sound logic. Barry—you fought this machine in Ollendor. Does it possess a vulnerability we can exploit?"

choice("Tell her to not have any secret meetings in rooms with crystals.", B3-Ch11a-Limitation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Limitation, v_b3_ch11_weakness = 0)
choice("Tell her about the Overseer's core.", B3-Ch11a-Limitation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Limitation, v_b3_ch11_weakness = 1)
choice("Tell her that the Overseer can only lie through omission.", B3-Ch11a-Limitation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Limitation, v_b3_ch11_weakness = 2)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Limitation
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch11_weakness == 0) {
"Avoid holding private councils in chambers lined with blue crystals," I say. "We had to take the same precautions in Ollendor to keep our plans confidential."

"An obvious measure, but thank you," Meridith says. "Given the prevalence of crystal formations in these shafts, complete privacy will be difficult. Does anyone have any suggestions?"

"It is a constraint rather than a weakness," Kate says, "but the construct appears limited to observation and speech through the crystals. It cannot cast offensive spells through the stone. Though if it interfaces with the node, its capabilities may expand."

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_weakness == 1 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
"Its only physical vulnerability is its primary core," I say. "That remains buried in Ollendor, and we don't have the exact chamber coordinates."

"Not immediately actionable, but noted," Meridith says. "Every detail matters. Other thoughts?"

"It is a constraint rather than a weakness," Kate says, "but the construct appears limited to observation and speech through the crystals. It cannot cast offensive spells through the stone. Though if it interfaces with the node, its capabilities may expand."

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_weakness == 1 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
"Its only known physical vulnerability was its core," I say. "We shattered that in Ollendor, yet its consciousness migrated into the mineral matrix anyway. Finding a new weak point requires understanding how it survived."

"Understood," Meridith says. "If you recall anything further, inform me. Any other observations?"

"It is a constraint rather than a weakness," Kate says, "but the construct appears limited to observation and speech through the crystals. It cannot cast offensive spells through the stone. Though if it interfaces with the node, its capabilities may expand."

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_weakness == 2 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 9) {
"The Overseer is incapable of direct falsehood," I say. "It can only deceive through omission."

"A rigid logic constraint?" Meridith muses. "Valuable, if true. How did you verify this? Did the machine declare it?"

"It did," I admit, "and its behavior matched. It constantly maneuvered around direct lies even when fabrication would have served it better."

"I remain skeptical, but I will factor it in," Meridith says. "Other insights?"

"It is a constraint rather than a weakness," Kate says, "but the construct appears limited to observation and speech through the crystals. It cannot cast offensive spells through the stone. Though if it interfaces with the node, its capabilities may expand."

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_weakness == 2 && v_b3_ch4_billy == 9) {
"The Overseer is incapable of direct falsehood," I say. "It can only deceive through omission."

"That rule dissolved when the rebel leader tampered with its protocols," Kate reminds me. "It stated outright that its truth restrictions were severed."

"Right," I catch myself. "Forgot about that override. Disregard."

"A pity," Meridith sighs. "Other thoughts?"

"It is a constraint rather than a weakness," Kate says, "but the construct appears limited to observation and speech through the crystals. It cannot cast offensive spells through the stone. Though if it interfaces with the node, its capabilities may expand."

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_path == 0 && v_b3_ch11_reverse == 0) {
"That aligns with my assessment," Meridith says. "Aura tethering requires no destructive magic. We will assume for now it cannot launch attacks from within the rock, though we remain vigilant."

"Never underestimate that machine," Daren warns. "It plays the sterile bureaucrat, but it schemes circles around most casters."

"I witnessed as much," Meridith nods. "It secured an alliance with my rivals within minutes of arrival. I will not make the mistake of taking it lightly."

"Have you considered treating with it directly?" Uverg asks. "The lore it holds—"

"The entity engineered this hostility to ingratiate itself with Heksol," Meridith cuts him off. "It has chosen its allegiance."

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_path == 1 || v_b3_ch11_reverse == 1) {
"That aligns with my assessment," Meridith says. "Aura tethering requires no destructive magic. We will assume for now it cannot launch attacks from within the rock, though we remain vigilant."

"Have you considered treating with it directly?" Uverg asks. "The lore it holds—"

"The entity engineered this hostility to ingratiate itself with Heksol," Meridith cuts him off. "It has chosen its allegiance."

}
"You provoked the breach by denying it access to the node," Uverg points out. "It sought out the only faction willing to bargain. A softer stance might—"

"No," Meridith says with finality. "Negotiations are done. Speak with it if you must, Uverg, but I classify the Overseer as an enemy combatant."

Uverg bows his head. "As you command, Lady Meridith."

"We have lingered here long enough," Meridith says. "Hadrik—your grievance is noted, but I require your companions' counsel on a matter of extreme importance. Will you join us?"

Hadrik sighs, stowing his remaining bottles in his pack. "I've said my piece. Let's hear what you have to say."

"Good," Meridith says. "Follow me."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Analysis, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Analysis)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Analysis
TEXT:

"Lady Meridith, a moment," Uverg calls out, shuffling toward us. "May I accompany you?"

"Can this wait?" Meridith asks, her hand already raised toward the junction. "I require privacy with our guests."

"It concerns the preliminary assay on the blue crystals," Uverg says, holding up a thick scroll of parchment. "I assumed you wanted the data promptly."

Meridith sighs softly. "Very well. Give your summary on the walk back, then return to your quarters."

"Understood," Uverg says with an earnest nod.

"Back toward the node chamber," Meridith directs, leading the way.

"See you around, Kleykarth," Hadrik calls over his shoulder.

"Later, dwarf," Kleykarth waves a lazy mug from the crate.

"So," Hadrik leans toward Meridith as we leave the checkpoint. "Did you actually murder Heksol like he said? Come clean."

"If you are trying to bait me, you are wasting your breath," Meridith replies without breaking her stride.

#if(v_b3_ch11_path == 0 && v_b3_ch11_reverse == 0) {
"She totally killed him," Hadrik whispers to us with a broad grin.

We walk for several minutes through the winding tunnels. Uverg fills the silence with a relentless, excruciatingly dry breakdown of mineral densities, harmonic frequencies, and crystal facets. Even with my years of theory study, half his technical terminology goes straight over my head. Meridith simply listens with a glazed, stoic patience.

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_path == 1 || v_b3_ch11_reverse == 1) {
"She totally killed him," Hadrik whispers to us with a broad grin.

We walk for several minutes through the winding tunnels. Uverg fills the silence with a relentless, excruciatingly dry breakdown of mineral densities, harmonic frequencies, and crystal facets. Even with my years of theory study, half his technical terminology goes straight over my head. Meridith simply listens with a glazed, stoic patience.

At the main junction, we meet Daren, Melindra, Flower, and Illuna, who are just returning from the wrong tunnel after sorting out their argument with the checkpoint guard. Meridith waves them in, and we continue as a group.

}
"Ahem," Uverg clears his throat as we proceed down the central shaft. "Moving beyond the basic structural assay, I will now highlight the central discovery from my twenty-page report. Most crucially: these formations are not purely blue. Under proper magnification, a faint indigo hue is discernible. This renders our historical nomenclature entirely inaccurate. Following further analysis, I propose officially renaming them 'blue with a slight tinge of indigo crystals.' History in the making."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Entrusted, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Entrusted)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Entrusted
TEXT:

"Uverg," Meridith says, maintaining a remarkably steady tone despite the twitch in her jaw. "While I appreciate the rigor of your research, when I assigned you this task, I hoped you would utilize your... specialized insight to examine the crystals, rather than mundane mineral tools. Do you follow?"

"Ah," Uverg's face clears. "You refer to the knowledge imparted by the Magium node."

"Exactly," Meridith says. "Conventional analysis cannot explain the awakening over the past few days. I need answers drawn from the source."

"Well," Uverg muses, scratching his chin through his wool sock. "I recall an analogous disturbance occurring roughly ten thousand years ago, back when humans and lessathi were a single race."

"Lessathi and humans were the same race?" Hadrik asks, stopping dead in his tracks. "Is that common knowledge down here?"

"No," Meridith says, startled. "This is entirely new. Uverg, are you certain this is Magium lore and not a dream?"

"Quite certain, Lady Meridith," Uverg says. "The distinction between common facts and cosmic memory blurs at times, but the provenance is clear."

"Are you claiming our ancestors were ordinary humans," Meridith asks, "and that something stripped the magic from our auras?"

"Gradually, over generations," Uverg explains. "The divergence began with subtle shifts in aura frequency until the bloodlines separated entirely."

"We share identical anatomy," Meridith murmurs, "yet our historical records contain no mention of this. Why would the elders conceal it?"

"Spite," Uverg says simply. "The proto-humans and proto-lessathi belonged to warring factions. Our ancestors dwelt underground, revering the Magium nodes as deities. The surface humans lured them into deep subterranean fissures under false pretenses, pulled up the ropes, and sealed the caverns above them. The trapped tribe nursed that hatred through centuries of dark isolation."

choice(""Is this why marriage between humans and lessathi is forbidden by your society?"", B3-Ch11a-Generations, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Generations, v_b3_ch11_escape = 0)
choice(""How did they escape? Did they manage to invent climbing tools?"", B3-Ch11a-Generations, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Generations, v_b3_ch11_escape = 1)
choice(""Since they worshipped the nodes, does this mean that the Magium spoke to them?"", B3-Ch11a-Generations, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Generations, v_b3_ch11_escape = 2)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Generations
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch11_escape == 0) {
"Is that why your society forbids human marriage?" I ask.

"The root of the tradition, yes," Uverg says. "There were eras of peace where the two peoples mingled freely, but the Still Winter revived hardline segregation under radical councils. Lady Meridith is among the first in centuries attempting to bridge the divide."

"You mentioned an earlier crystal awakening," Meridith steers the topic back. "Was it tied to the aura mutation?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_escape == 1) {
"How did they escape? Did they manage to invent climbing tools?" I ask.

"A teleportation lattice," Uverg says.

"A teleporter?" Hadrik raises an eyebrow. "With cave materials?"

"It took centuries," Uverg says. "Decades just to identify edible subterranean fungi and map the chasm networks. Rapid innovation began only after they learned to extract schematics from the Magium nodes."

"You mentioned an earlier crystal awakening," Meridith steers the topic back. "Was it tied to the aura mutation?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_escape == 2) {
"Did the Magium speak to them while they worshipped it?" I ask.

"Not for the first century," Uverg says. "The worship was born of terror. Watching their kin lose their minds upon touching the nodes convinced them an omnipotent god demanded submission."

"You mentioned an earlier crystal awakening," Meridith steers the topic back. "Was it tied to the aura mutation?"

}
"Prolonged exposure to the awakened mineral field altered their auras," Uverg says. "An underground blast triggered the resonance, and the resonance took over a century to subside. I can only hope this modern resurgence does not distort our physiology further."

"Generational drift is a problem for later," Meridith says. "What about immediate effects? Do the records mention how to quiet them?"

"They went dormant on their own once the resonance bled off," Uverg says. "No active countermeasure existed."

"A start, at least," Meridith says. "Thank you, Uverg."

"How did surface humans react when the lessathi finally dug their way out?" Diane asks.

"With open arms," Uverg replies. "Centuries had passed; surface dwellers had forgotten the feud entirely. But our ancestors remembered every grievance. They feigned friendship, traded Magium-derived inventions, and integrated into high society while waiting for the optimal moment to strike."

"What was the strike?" Daren asks.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Eccentric, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Eccentric)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Eccentric
TEXT:

"A coordinated coup," Uverg says. "Lessathi inventors held key administrative and technological posts. They overthrew the human kings in a single night, enslaved the populace, and expanded outward until an empire spanned Varathia. Centuries later, reformist rulers emancipated the humans into second-class subjects—a status that endured until the empire fell during the Still Winter."

"Ten thousand years of oppression over an ancient grudge," Daren mutters, shaking his head in sorrow. "Your people hold onto hatred."

"We have long memories," Uverg agrees mildly.

"Uverg," Hadrik speaks up, scratching his beard. "A personal question. You're clearly sharp, so why the boot on your head and socks on your hands?"

"A social experiment," Uverg says cheerfully. "Most people who touch the node go completely mad. By adopting the garb of a lunatic while speaking with rigorous logic, I test who possesses the discernment to look past surface eccentricities. Almost no one manages it. Fascinating study of perception, wouldn't you say?"

"If that's what keeps you occupied," Hadrik shrugs.

"It is not merely the wardrobe, Uverg," Meridith says softly. "Your thought patterns shifted. You never cared so little for the opinion of others before the node. It is as if part of your mind remained behind in the Magium realm."

"I feel no different," Uverg says. "Only possessed of vast lore and an insatiable desire for more. If eccentricity is the price of enlightenment, I pay it gladly."

"I know," Meridith sighs. "You may conduct your research as you see fit. Now, if you have nothing further, I require a private conference with my guests."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Adventure, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Adventure)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Adventure
TEXT:

"Of course," Uverg bows. "I shall complete the written report for your desk."

"Please do," Meridith says.

"Farewell, travelers," Uverg waves a sock-covered hand and wanders back down the passage, his bare feet silent on the rock.

"Strange man," Hadrik observes. "I like him."

"As do I," Meridith murmurs, watching him disappear into the gloom. She turns back to face us. "Now. Why I called you together."

She outlines the revised arrangement: participate in the tournament, keep clearing regional objectives, and receive node access if their strike eliminates Eiden without our involvement. If Eiden survives, the choice to fight him or walk away remains ours, with no advance pledge required.

Hadrik stares at her in sheer disbelief.

"That's it?" he barks. "We just keep playing the tournament and you hand over the prize for free? No murder pledge? I don't buy it. What's the catch?"

"You only receive access without fighting if our strike succeeds," Meridith reiterates. "If he survives, you must finish him to claim the node."

"You're still offering a free prize for what I was already doing," Hadrik says. "I already told you I won't finish a crippled opponent. You get nothing from me. Why offer me access?"

"Traveling with your companions keeps them alive," Meridith says evenly. "And as long as they survive, the possibility remains that some of them will choose to strike when the moment comes. Eliminating Eiden has been our civilization's purpose for six centuries. I will use every thread."

"Well," Hadrik rubs his beard, grinning slightly. "I'm in this tournament for the adventure, not the prize. If you want to reward me for doing what I was already planning to do, fine. I'm in."

"I am glad to hear it," Meridith says. "Does the rest of the group agree?"

"As long as we aren't bound to assassinate Eiden, yes," Daren says, while Illuna nods behind him. "We can agree to that."

choice(""Agreed. It's not like I was going to give up on the tournament after coming this far."", B3-Ch11a-Natural, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Natural, v_b3_ch11_money = 0, v_b3_ch11_magic = 1)
choice(""Hey, speak for yourself. Eiden is my best buddy. I'm not planning to betray him."", B3-Ch11a-Natural, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Natural, v_b3_ch11_money = 1, v_b3_ch11_magic = 1)
choice(""You know... the Magium is great and all, but I could also use some extra money."", B3-Ch11a-Natural, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Natural, v_b3_ch11_money = 2, v_b3_ch11_magic = 1)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Natural
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch11_money == 0) {
"Agreed. It's not like I was going to give up on the tournament after coming this far."

"Excellent," Meridith says. "A necessary compromise."

"So now that we've agreed on... whatever this is," Hadrik says, "do we get the pinecone detector?"

"One moment," Meridith says. "There is one final matter before you depart."

"What?" Daren asks.

"A demonstration," Meridith says. "A taste of the Magium node, so you understand the magnitude of what you stand to gain."

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_money == 1) {
"Hey, speak for yourself. Eiden is my best buddy. I'm not planning to betray him."

"What?" Meridith blinks, taken aback. "You agreed in private—"

"Ignore Barry," Daren sighs, rubbing his forehead. "He's being sarcastic. He does this."

"I never joke," I say deadpan. "Always serious."

"Best to tune him out," Daren advises Meridith.

"Right," Meridith says, still visibly uncertain.

"So now that we've agreed on... whatever this is," Hadrik says, "do we get the pinecone detector?"

"One moment," Meridith says. "There is one final matter before you depart."

"What?" Daren asks.

"A demonstration," Meridith says. "A taste of the Magium node, so you understand the magnitude of what you stand to gain."

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_money == 2) {
"You know... the Magium is great and all, but I could also use some extra money."

"Gold in addition to the node?" Meridith's brow arches. "Deliver the killing blow to Eiden when the time comes, and you will have more gold than you can carry."

"Deal," I say.

"Splendid," Meridith nods.

"So now that we've agreed on... whatever this is," Hadrik says, "do we get the pinecone detector?"

"One moment," Meridith says. "There is one final matter before you depart."

"What?" Daren asks.

"A demonstration," Meridith says. "A taste of the Magium node, so you understand the magnitude of what you stand to gain."

}
"Does this node come with an instruction manual?" Daren asks. "Because most of us wouldn't know where to begin."

"We utilize Magium connectors," Meridith says. "Buffer instruments that extract knowledge safely. The process requires patience, but the possibilities are boundless. I brought one along for a demonstration."

"Whoa, whoa," Arraka's voice squeaks from the amulet, a tremor running through the gold metal. "You idiots aren't marching back into that chamber, are you?"

"Why, Arraka," Hadrik smirks down at the pendant. "Sounding a little nervous, aren't we?"

"Shut your mouth, you undersized circus freak!" Arraka shrieks. "I wasn't addressing you!"

"No shame in fear," Hadrik chuckles. "Completely natural."

"I swear, one more word and I'll—"

A sharp series of rhythmic chimes cuts her off, vibrating intensely against my hip.

"What was that?" Arraka snaps.

"Your stat booster," Meridith says, turning to me with a faint smile. "The magical pathways have finished compiling their first level."

The moment the chime fades, a sudden, searing warmth floods outward from my chest. It isn't the raging, violent inferno of Emergency Mode that threatened to rip my heart apart; it is a clean, humming current flowing through my arms, pooling in my palms and fingertips. For the first time in twenty-eight years, I feel the ambient magical plane brushing against my soul—not as an abstract theory in a textbook, but as a real, tangible tether waiting for my command.

My breath catches in my throat. My hands are trembling, but not from pain.

"Let's see if the flight technique works," I murmur, my voice slightly unsteady.

"Flight requires advanced stat thresholds," Meridith begins, frowning. "Level one only supports basic spark—wait. How are you doing that?"

Air currents whip violently around my boots with a sharp hiss. With a clumsy, breathless lurch, I rise two feet off the stone floor, wobbling precariously as the updraft buffets against the seredium walls.

"Just generalist air currents," I call down, struggling to balance my center of gravity against the swirling draft. "Treat it like steering a glider."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Connector, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Connector)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Connector
TEXT:

"Not bad, Barry," Melindra calls up with a smirk, crossing her arms. "A bit rougher than my draft, but you're airborne."

"Barely," I wheeze, fighting the sway as the air slips wildly under my boots. "The emergency surge gave me tighter control. I'll need weeks of practice before I take this into a fight."

"You cast that using only level one in both magic stats?" Meridith asks, staring upward in astonishment. "I expected nothing beyond minor sparks until you reached the second tier."

"Theory and combinations," I tell her, bracing against the gust. "Individually they're small, but layered together, they get dangerous fast. I've only skimmed the index, and the versatility is staggering."

"Then unlocking them was the right decision," Meridith says. "Come down, please. We enter the chamber together."

"Right," I say, cutting the draft and landing with a heavy, uncoordinated thud that rattles my teeth.

Meridith taps her wand to the wall. The seredium slab sinks back, opening the way into the node chamber.

We step into the vast cavern. The massive blue sphere pulses with slow, rhythmic light. Meridith withdraws an instrument resembling my booster, studded with multicolored buttons.

"A Magium connector," Meridith explains, holding it up. "A buffer interface. For your first attempt, press only the green toggle. The red toggle is for experienced operators and can cause severe disorientation. The remaining controls govern specialized filters we will not explore today. Who is first?"

"I am," Daren steps forward immediately.

"Keep it under sixty seconds," Meridith instructs, handing him the device. "Even with the buffer, prolonged exposure strains an unconditioned mind."

"What of the fragment?" Illuna asks abruptly.

"Pardon?" Meridith pauses.

"The entity you call Creator," Illuna says. "Does it linger in this room?"

"The Creator departs and returns as it wills," Meridith says. "Did you wish to speak with it?"

"Hardly," Illuna says. "You hear that, Arraka? The chamber is clear. Cease your fidgeting."

"I am a spirit bound to metal!" Arraka snarls from the amulet. "How does one fidget without limbs?"

"Your aura has been vibrating like a plucked lute string since we entered the shaft," Illuna remarks coldly.

"Oh, an aura master now?" Arraka sneers. "Shall I take lessons from you?"

"Whenever you like," Illuna replies evenly.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Endless, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Endless)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Endless
TEXT:

"Green button, correct?" Daren asks, positioning his thumb.

"Press and observe," Meridith nods.

Daren depresses the stud. His eyes widen.

"This is... extraordinary," he breathes. "Every concept, every spell structure... it feels an arm's reach away. The harder I reach, the further it recedes, yet I feel as if a few more seconds would bring complete clarity. It is mesmerizing."

"The initial threshold," Meridith says. "The connector demonstrates the scope, but extracting usable schematics takes months of dedicated study."

"I see the discipline required," Daren says, clicking the toggle off. The glow leaves his face. "Tempting to lose oneself in it. But the potential... tens of thousands of healing paradigms alone. Coming to Varathia was not in vain."

"My turn," I say, stepping up.

"Go ahead," Daren hands the unit over. "You'll appreciate this."

I face the sphere and click the green toggle.

Instantly, my consciousness expands into an infinite, silent archive. Uncounted volumes stretching into eternity, indexing every law of nature from the birth of the cosmos. Yet an invisible barrier holds me at the entrance. The restraint is deliberate—a protective ceiling built into the device to shield fragile minds from annihilation.

#if(v_b3_ch10_node == 1) {
Having felt the raw node directly, I recognize this green channel as a curated sliver.

The real conduit must be the red toggle.

}
#if(v_b3_ch10_node == 0) {
The green toggle is merely a safe observation window. The real conduit must be the red toggle.

}
choice("Don't press the red button.", B3-Ch11a-Generous, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Generous, v_b3_ch11_red = 0)
choice("Press the red button.", B3-Ch11a-Generous, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Generous, v_b3_ch11_red = 1)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Generous
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch11_taste,1) if (v_b3_ch11_red == 1)
achievement("A small taste of the big prize",v_ac_b3_ch11_taste)
#if(v_b3_ch11_red == 0) {
I heed Meridith's caution and leave the red toggle alone. After a few more moments skimming the unreachable shelves, I click the green stud, cutting the feed.

"Well?" Meridith asks.

"A tease," I say. "Fascinating, but nothing concrete."

"Do not despair," Meridith says. "Our researchers spend weeks isolating a single theorem. A minute is merely a glance."

"Fair enough," I say.

Kate takes the device next, followed by Hadrik. Both hand it back with respectful nods, though their eyes lack the hunger Daren and I felt. Melindra politely refuses; whatever her grievances with Eiden, she will not participate in a ritual designed to buy his death.

Flower steps up last. As her finger touches the casing, her irises shift from green to cold, brilliant blue as Illuna takes the helm.

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_red == 1) {
Ignoring Meridith's warning, my thumb slams down on the red toggle.

"Not the red!" Meridith's shout reaches me from an impossible distance.

The quiet archive shatters. A roaring deluge of raw cosmic data hammers my consciousness like a torrential river. Concepts tear past in violent currents—too fast to parse, too vast to contain. My skull feels ready to split.

Meridith was right. It is too much. I need to disengage.

No. Not empty-handed.

I force every shred of mental discipline toward a single, simple concept: air manipulation. Stability. Vector balance. I lock onto the stream, tear out the formula, and smash the red button.

The connection snaps.

"Barry!" Flower is kneeling in front of me, eyes wide with alarm.

My knees knock together; tremors wrack my arms and shoulders from the shock of the influx. I force air into my lungs, steadying my hands.

"I'm fine," I wheeze, straightening up. "Quite a rush."

"Warnings are wasted on you," Meridith says, crossing her arms in exasperation. "I trust you derived some lesson from that folly."

"I did," I say. I channel mana into my boots. A cushion of air lifts me smoothly off the floor—steady, balanced, completely stable. "Flight vectors calibrated."

"I intended a lesson in prudence, but results speak," Meridith sighs. "Who is next?"

Kate takes the unit, followed by Hadrik. Both hand it back impressed but detached. Melindra declines outright, unwilling to take part in anything aimed at Eiden's death.

Flower steps forward last. As her fingers close around the metal, her eyes flash from warm green to icy blue.

}
"Allow me," Illuna says, her fingers dancing across the red toggle and several lateral switches in rapid sequence.

Meridith stiffens, watching in silence until Illuna clicks the device off and hands it back.

"You have operated one of these before," Meridith notes.

"Our previous unit was damaged," Illuna replies coolly. "Replacements are difficult to source. I thank you for the access."

"Had I known, I would not have permitted it," Meridith says dryly. "Did you extract what you required?"

"More than anticipated," Illuna says. "It will refine several techniques I am developing."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Exploring, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Exploring)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Exploring
TEXT:

"Splendid," Meridith says, stowing the device. "That concludes our demonstration. I trust you now grasp what our alliance offers. Again, no immediate vow is required, but—"

"Meridith," a resonant voice echoes from within the blue sphere. "Approach."

Meridith halts, straightening instantly. "Yes, Creator."

"Aura vibrating again, Arraka," Illuna notes.

"Shut up," Arraka snarls.

"Only Meridith," a second voice speaks from the sphere, older and sharper. "No outsiders."

"Of course," Meridith turns to us. "Wait in the outer passage for a moment."

"We'll give you privacy," Daren says, ushering us toward the exit.

"Gladly," Hadrik mutters as we step through and the seredium wall grinds shut behind us.

"Do we wait, or look around?" Hadrik asks, glancing down the empty hallway.

"Stay here," Diane advises. "You were fortunate with the checkpoint guards. Other lessathi are looking for excuses to provoke a fight, especially with Kate carrying Heldenbusorag's relic."

"Suits me," I say, pulling out my booster. "Gives me time to study this new spell index. I recognize several formulas Brinhorn used during my apprenticeship. With a few modifications—"

"Greetings, friends," a metallic voice chimes from a blue crystal embedded in the ceiling. "The Overseer protocol offers apologies for its previous departure. Disregarding your presence was uncharacteristically discourteous."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Recording, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Recording)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Recording
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch11_aftermath,1) if (v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1)
achievement("Hidden alliance aftermath",v_ac_b3_ch11_aftermath)
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
"Friends?" Hadrik scoffs, crossing his arms. "I smashed your metal chassis to scrap back in the palace."

"Processing..." the Overseer hums, the faceted crystal vibrating with monotone precision. "I neglected that certain biological units struggle with irony. To clarify: I hold no affection for your party. In fact, I anticipate your demise with measurable satisfaction. However, analytical modeling suggests mutual benefit in eroding your trust in Meridith. I took the liberty of recording her communion with the node."

"Rusty tactics, machine," Daren says, frowning upward. "You tried playing selective audio back in Ollendor. You're going to feed us an out-of-context snippet."

The Overseer ignores him. Sound crackles through the crystal with a sharp hiss of static.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 7 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 8) {
"What do you want?" Daren asks bluntly, looking up into the ceiling glow.

"First, a status update: the seredium towers in Ollendor are undergoing optimization," the Overseer drones. "To compensate for output deficits, additional mage captives are being integrated into the matrix. No gratitude is necessary."

"You heartless monster," Kate's fists clench white at her sides, frost sparking on her gloves. "You came here to gloat about slaves?"

"A secondary report," the Overseer continues without pause. "My primary purpose is warning you regarding Meridith. Her interests do not align with yours. I recorded her current consultation with the node for your review."

"Hard to trust anything you play after that," Daren says coldly.

The Overseer ignores him. Sound crackles through the crystal with a sharp hiss of static.

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8 && v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
"Is that your only purpose?" Daren asks warily, glancing at the glowing stone.

"Negative," the Overseer replies. "Research into the seredium towers progresses efficiently. I project the first cohort of prisoners will be emancipated within a fortnight without destabilizing municipal power."

"Good," Kate says, relaxing her posture slightly.

"Second, a warning concerning Meridith," the Overseer says. "Her alignment with your objectives is superficial. I recorded her dialogue with the node to illustrate."

Sound crackles through the crystal as the playback begins.

}
"Creator, with respect, you underestimate Eiden," Meridith's voice echoes from the stone, taut and urgent. "His message was unambiguous: 'remember the still winter.' He anticipates our move and is weaving countermeasures across the continent. If we hesitate, our window closes forever."

"What do you propose?" a creaking elder voice responds from the recording, resonant as grinding millstones.

"Accelerate the timetable. Finalize preparations within days—"

"No!" a harsh, youthful voice cuts through the elder's tone like a whip crack. "The timeline is fixed. Premature execution guarantees failure. Is that clear?"

"...Yes, Creator," Meridith yields reluctantly. "I understand."

The recording cuts off with a dull click.

"As demonstrated," the Overseer states, "Meridith comprehends Eiden's warning far better than she admitted. Furthermore, preliminary analysis indicates her strike encompasses targets beyond the stillwater."

"Diane," Kate turns to her friend, searching her eyes. "Do you know how she intends to kill Eiden?"

"I don't," Diane admits, shaking her head. "Those who know the plan are confined to the deep shafts to prevent Eiden reading it from their auras. I doubt Jason knows either, and Meridith confides in him far more than me."

"Who is Jason?" Hadrik asks.

"Diane's brother," Kate says. "Where is he? I hoped to see him before we left."

"On assignment on the surface," Diane says. "We might cross paths on the road to the sacred forest. I'll contact him to see if he can disclose his route."

"Wait," Kate blinks, surprise softening her guarded expression. "You're traveling with us?"

"Meridith was dispatching me to Galamir anyway," Diane smiles warmly. "I persuaded her to let me join your escort since we share the road. I hope that's all right."

choice(""Not at all. A friend of Kate's is a friend of ours!"", B3-Ch11a-Detector, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Detector, v_b3_ch11_diane = 0)
choice(""Only if you promise not to shock us with your electric powers again."", B3-Ch11a-Detector, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Detector, v_b3_ch11_diane = 1)
choice(""I'm sorry, but it kind of sounds to me like Meridith is sending you to spy on us."", B3-Ch11a-Detector, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Detector, v_b3_ch11_diane = 2)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Detector
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch11_diane == 0) {
"Not at all. A friend of Kate's is a friend of ours!"

"Glad to hear it," Diane smiles. "I know our introduction was rocky, but we'll have ample time to clear the air on the road to Galamir."

"Meridith concludes her communion," the Overseer announces from above. "I shall withdraw before further friction arises. Bear this in mind: Meridith is not your ally. Trusting her will prove fatal. Farewell."

The crystal's illumination dims to a dull gray.

"And he's gone," Hadrik grunts. "Anyone willing to wager that's the last we see of him?"

"I won't take that bet," Daren says. "He has an ear in every seam. Assume we're monitored whenever blue rock is in sight."

The stone slab grinds open with a low rumble. Meridith steps through into the corridor, adjusting the cuffs of her sleeves.

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_diane == 1) {
"Only if you promise not to shock us with your electric powers again."

"No lightning at all?" Diane pouts playfully. "Not even between comrades?"

"Let's manage comrades first," I say. "Then we'll negotiate the voltage."

"Fair enough," Diane laughs softly.

"Meridith concludes her communion," the Overseer announces from above. "I shall withdraw before further friction arises. Bear this in mind: Meridith is not your ally. Trusting her will prove fatal. Farewell."

The crystal's illumination dims to a dull gray.

"And he's gone," Hadrik grunts. "Anyone willing to wager that's the last we see of him?"

"I won't take that bet," Daren says. "He has an ear in every seam. Assume we're monitored whenever blue rock is in sight."

The stone slab grinds open with a low rumble. Meridith steps through into the corridor, adjusting the cuffs of her sleeves.

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_diane == 2) {
"I'm sorry, but it kind of sounds to me like Meridith is sending you to spy on us."

"Barry, really?" Daren groans, shaking his head. "Tact, man."

"No, the suspicion is entirely warranted," Diane says calmly. "Childhood history with Kate doesn't buy unearned trust. I hope a few days on the march prove my intentions."

"Meridith concludes her communion," the Overseer announces from above. "I shall withdraw before further friction arises. Bear this in mind: Meridith is not your ally. Trusting her will prove fatal. Farewell."

The crystal's illumination dims to a dull gray.

"And he's gone," Hadrik grunts. "Anyone willing to wager that's the last we see of him?"

"I won't take that bet," Daren says. "He has an ear in every seam. Assume we're monitored whenever blue rock is in sight."

The stone slab grinds open with a low rumble. Meridith steps through into the corridor, adjusting the cuffs of her sleeves.

}
"Apologies for the delay," Meridith says, sealing the passage behind her. "The Creator demanded urgent council. Now, let us collect your tracker so you can begin."

"FINALLY," Arraka shrieks from the pendant, her voice ringing off the stone. "I thought we'd rot down in this hole."

"Did Diane inform you?" Meridith asks, ignoring the amulet completely.

"That she's accompanying us to the sacred forest and Galamir? Yes," Kate says.

"Splendid," Meridith says. "Her detour will not impact the summit; diplomatic delegations across the continent are delayed. Follow me. I cached the detector in a transit corridor."

"Diplomatic envoy for what?" Daren asks, falling in beside her. "What summit?"

"Tyrath has declared total war against every non-monster settlement on Varathia," Meridith says grimly. "His vanguard was marching on the capitals when his elite orc commander persuaded him to convene negotiations first. Tyrath's opening demand will likely be unconditional surrender, but the talks buy time."

"Diane represents the lessathi, and the orc represents Tyrath," Daren says, rubbing his chin thoughtfully. "Who else attends?"

"The golden fox dispatches Leo the leopard," Meridith says, tapping a wall to reveal a side passage. "The mountain wyrms send an independent emissary, having never recognized Tyrath's crown. General Fyron attends for the goblin tribes. The King of Galamir mediates, while the remaining monarchs attend via projection crystals."

"Quite a gathering," Daren murmurs. "Leo, the orc captain, Fyron... we know half the table."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Declaration, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Declaration)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Declaration
TEXT:

"Acquaintance is not friendship," Meridith cautions, her pace steady. "I doubt any of them harbor goodwill toward you."

"Fyron is an ally, more or less," I say. "Right, Illuna?"

"...By no conventional definition," Illuna replies dryly from Flower's mouth, "but maintain the delusion if it comforts your fragile nerves."

"He won't turn on us without cause," I insist.

"He will turn the exact moment it serves his ledger," Illuna counters.

"Right," I mutter. "Good talk."

"Galamir serves as both the tournament rally point and the war summit," Daren notes thoughtfully. "No coincidence, surely. Is Galamir the continental capital?"

"Not officially, but in terms of real influence, yes," Meridith explains. "Through crushing debt, clandestine manipulation, and ancient treaties, the King of Galamir holds the other monarchs in the palm of his hand. Tread carefully in his domain. Slaying a corrupt noble in an obscure province went unnoticed, but touching nobility in Galamir brings instant disqualification and permanent banishment from the continent."

"Understood," Daren says. "Good to know."

"I assume Eiden granted you the bypass allowing you to strike Varathian locals?" Meridith asks, glancing back.

"He did," I say. "In Thilias, after our encounter with the Earl."

"Predictable," Meridith sighs. She taps a wall panel, exposing a narrow recess containing a slender metal slate.

"Speaking of Eiden," I say as we halt. "What did his message actually signify? 'Remember the still winter'—we couldn't settle on a meaning."

"Is that not obvious?" Meridith lifts the slate from the alcove, her expression stark and grim. "It is a declaration of war."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Offered, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Offered)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Offered
TEXT:

Meridith taps the wall panel shut and hands the slate to Kate.

"Your pinecone detector," Meridith says. "It is simple to operate. Diane can explain the sensory readings."

"Thank you," Kate says, sliding the device carefully into her pack.

"My obligations are met," Meridith says. "Urgent matters require my attention—several involving our uninvited mechanical guest."

"We appreciate your aid and the demonstration," Daren says, offering a formal nod. "You have given us much to weigh."

"I expect your answer when the hour arrives," Meridith says, fixing us with a sharp look. "Do not squander the investment I made in you today. Look after Diane."

"We will," Kate promises quietly.

"Farewell," Meridith steps into a side tunnel, the shadows swallowing her robes in an instant.

"This way to the transit chamber," Diane takes the lead, striding toward the junction. "Straight path from here."

A minute later, we enter the circular teleportation nexus. The Heksol engineer glares sullenly at Heldenbusorag's scepter strapped to Kate's back, but holds his tongue and looks to Diane for direction.

"Return us to the surface insertion point," Diane instructs.

"Standing by," the technician drones, keying the activation runes along the pedestal.

White light erupts from the floor in a blinding column. The harmonic hum vibrates deep through my marrow, and then the subterranean gloom tears away into crisp, cool mountain air and rustling pine boughs bathed in afternoon sun.

Before anyone can take two steps across the grass, Melindra channels mana into a rising updraft, hovering a foot above the meadow.

"Well," Melindra says with a casual wave. "This is where I split."

choice(""Really?... Just like that?"", B3-Ch11a-Afford, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Afford, v_b3_ch11_melindra = 0)
choice(""Hey, you're leaving and not even going to give me a rock as a souvenir?"", B3-Ch11a-Afford, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Afford, v_b3_ch11_melindra = 1)
choice(""Alright, goodbye."", B3-Ch11a-Afford, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Afford, v_b3_ch11_melindra = 2)
choice(""You know... I'm actually going to miss you."", B3-Ch11a-Afford, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Afford, v_b3_ch11_melindra = 3)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Afford
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch11_melindra == 0) {
"Really?... Just like that?"

"Just like that," Melindra says, her voice breezy even as Flower begins sniffling softly behind me. "I don't do drawn-out goodbyes. We have objectives to finish. We can cross paths once the dust settles."

"Sooner than that," Kate points out, stepping closer to the breeze of her draft. "The God of Time's ritual, remember?"

"Right," Melindra's brow lifts with a brief smile. "Forgot the timing."

"Are you certain we cannot assist with your brother?" Daren asks, concern furrowing his brow. "If you share details—"

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_melindra == 1) {
"Hey, you're leaving and not even going to give me a rock as a souvenir?"

"Since you asked so nicely," Melindra smirks, tossing a smooth, flat river stone from her pouch. Behind me, Flower sniffles loudly. "Six centuries ago, those were carved into fortune wards. You need all the luck you can carry."

"Thoughtful," I say, catching the weight in my palm and tucking it into my pocket.

"Don't lose it," she warns, pointing a finger. "I want it back when the tournament ends."

"Naturally," I grin.

"We'll see each other sooner than that," Kate reminds her. "The God of Time's ritual."

"Right," Melindra says, her expression softening. "Forgot the timing."

"Are you certain we cannot assist with your brother?" Daren asks, concern furrowing his brow. "If you share details—"

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_melindra == 2) {
"Alright, goodbye."

"Wait—goodbye?" Hadrik looks between us, bewildered, while Flower sniffles in the background. "Just like that? Leaving for good?"

"Not forever," Melindra says, hovering lightly on the wind. "You have your pinecone hunt; I have family to track. We'll cross paths."

"At the Time God's ritual, if not before," Kate adds, her arms folded.

"Right," Melindra nods. "Forgot the timing."

"Are you certain we cannot assist with your brother?" Daren asks, concern furrowing his brow. "If you share details—"

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_melindra == 3) {
"You know... I'm actually going to miss you."

"I... suppose I'll miss you too," Melindra says, her usual teasing mockery slipping into rare sincerity as Flower sniffles behind us. "All of you. It has been centuries since I traveled in company, but it wasn't terrible. We'll reconnect when our business is done."

"At the Time God's ritual," Kate says quietly.

"Right," Melindra says, looking at her with genuine warmth. "Forgot the timing."

"Are you certain we cannot assist with your brother?" Daren asks, concern furrowing his brow. "If you share details—"

}
"You've done enough," Melindra says, shaking her head. "I keep the details quiet because I don't know the full truth myself, and hunting real leads beats inventing nightmares. He fell in with dangerous people who sold him noble lies, and he vanished. I hold solid tracks now, and with the ring recovered from Ollendor's late king, I should reach him in days."

"Multiple searchers cover ground faster," Kate presses, taking another step forward.

"I fly," Melindra says simply. "Solo travel is three times faster. Focus on your objectives. I will transmit once I have him."

"Keep your transceiver active," Kate orders, her dark eyes fixed on her with unyielding intensity. "I will be calling whether it suits you or not."

"I would like that, icey," Melindra smiles warmly. "Truly."

"Do you really have to go?" Flower's voice wavers, tears spilling freely down her flushed cheeks. "Can't you stay just a few more days? We had so much left to do!"

"I cannot delay, Flower," Melindra says gently, her draft lowering until she is at eye level. "I wish I could take you, but your place is with your friends. I will miss you most of all."

"Can I call you?" Flower rubs her nose with the back of her wrist. "Constantly?"

"Whenever you like," Melindra promises. "And the rituals will reunite us soon."

"All right," Flower sniffles, wiping her face on her sleeve. "I can manage that."

"Well," Melindra glances at each of us in turn, her gaze lingering for a heartbeat. "This is the break."

"Aye," Hadrik rumbles, touching his chest. "Safe winds, lass."

"Fly slowly at first," Daren cautions. "The enchanter's geas should be satisfied now that we hold the detector, but take no chances with the binding."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Military, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Military)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Military
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer == 1 && v_b3_ch10_benefits == 1) {
"The geas isn't tugging," Melindra says, "but I'll keep the altitude low at first. Thanks for the thought."

"Safe travels," Daren nods.

"One last thing," Melindra looks down at me, a sharp smile touching her lips. "Barry—you haven't forgotten our post-tournament partnership, I trust?"

"Not a chance," I say.

"Good," she says. "Keep yourself in one piece, then. We have ground to cover together. Try leashing those defective survival instincts long enough to see the end of this."

"I'll do my best," I say.

"You'd better," she calls down, catching a powerful updraft. She waves once as the wind sweeps her above the pine canopy, rising into the azure sky until her silhouette blurs into the mountain distance.

Before the rustling draft even settles in the grass, Flower unclips her transceiver, dials the frequency, and taps the rune.

"Hey, Melindra! How's flying?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer == 0 || v_b3_ch10_benefits == 0) {
"The geas isn't tugging," Melindra says, "but I'll keep the altitude low at first. Thanks for the thought."

"Safe travels," Daren nods.

"See you in the clouds," Melindra calls down, catching a powerful updraft. She waves once as the wind sweeps her above the pine canopy, rising into the azure sky until her silhouette blurs into the mountain distance.

Before the rustling draft even settles in the grass, Flower unclips her transceiver, dials the frequency, and taps the rune.

"Hey, Melindra! How's flying?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch8_energizer == 0 || v_b2_ch8_energizer == 1 || v_b3_ch10_benefits == 0) {
"...Flower," Melindra's exasperated voice crackles from the speaker. "When I said call often, I didn't mean before I cleared the clearing. Never mind. I'm fine. How are you?"

"Doing great! Safe travels, bye!" Flower chirps, clicking the link shut.

She turns on her heel, beaming at our stunned faces.

"What are we waiting for? Pinecones aren't going to collect themselves! Double time! Hup, two, three, four! Hup, two, three, four!"

She marches into our backs, pushing us playfully down the dirt track. We manage barely a minute of forced marching before the air thins with an eerie, familiar pressure.

Hovering a foot above the road ahead, arms folded casually inside his loose sleeves, Eiden waits with his eyes serenely shut and a faint, polite smile on his lips.

"Greetings, friends," Eiden's voice drifts over the grass, light as a morning breeze. "Did you enjoy your descent into the lessathi warrens? How fares Meridith?"

Diane's fingers spark instantly with crackling blue lightning. Kate grabs her wrist without looking, forcing her hand down behind her cloak.

"If you're looking for Melindra, you just missed her," Daren says, stepping forward to shield the group. "She took flight a minute ago."

"No matter," Eiden dismisses lightly. "We will cross paths in due time. But she is not the purpose of my visit."

"Let me guess," I say, stepping up beside Daren. "The message."

"Sharp as ever, Barry," Eiden smiles. "Did you deliver it?"

"Directly to Meridith," I say.

"Splendid. And her response?"

"She didn't give one," I say.

"No response?" The smile falters slightly, a flicker of genuine surprise crossing his closed eyelids. "Nothing? Did she speak on its meaning?"

"She called it a declaration of war," I tell him.

"Ah," Eiden's shoulders sink in quiet, heavy disappointment. "I see."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Continent, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Continent)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Continent
TEXT:

"That wasn't what you meant?" I ask. "If you want to rephrase, I could head back down—"

"The phrasing was exact," Eiden says softly, his voice dropping all trace of performance. "She understood every syllable. That she chose that interpretation is deeply disappointing. I had hoped Meridith possessed more breadth of mind than her predecessors—that she might seek to avoid needless bloodshed. It appears I misjudged."

Silence settles over the mountain road, heavy, cold, and still.

"Did you accept her proposition?" Eiden asks plainly.

"What proposition?" I ask, my pulse spiking against my ribs.

"To strike me down in exchange for their node," Eiden says, his head tilting slightly. "Why else invite you into the deep vaults? Perhaps we might dispense with formalities and settle it now. You've honed your skills these past weeks. Surely the thought of killing me crossed your mind once or twice."

"Hold on," Daren steps between us, his broad frame tense. "We agreed to nothing. She made the proposal, but left the decision open until the tournament concludes."

"Ah," Eiden's smile returns, smooth, polite, and completely impenetrable. "Prudent. Accepting immediately would have been reckless, knowing I read deceit from auras. Take all the time you require. After all, only the fate of an entire continent hangs in the balance."

"Continent?" I counter. "Don't you mean island?"

Eiden turns his closed eyes toward me. "You know, Barry... I suspect I return to your company primarily for these wildly inappropriate remarks. You bring color to an otherwise dreary homecoming."

"Happy to provide a service," I say.

"Indeed," Eiden murmurs, pausing in brief contemplation. "Like Meridith, I shall await your conclusion with keen interest. Our next encounter will likely be in Galamir. I look forward to observing how you navigate the... rather peculiar circumstances of that city. Farewell for now."

A soft, sharp displacement of air, and he is gone.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Ending, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Ending)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Ending
TEXT:

"Arraka," I ask, scanning the empty treeline. "Is he gone, or just invisible?"

"Gone," Arraka snaps from the pendant. "Stop asking every time. I'll yell if he lingers."

"Right."

"Is this your standard routine with him?" Diane asks, staring wide-eyed at the empty space where Eiden hovered. "You spoke to him as if he were a traveling merchant."

"You get used to it," Daren says, adjusting his shield strap with a weary sigh. "Which way to the woods?"

"Northwest," Diane points toward the timbered ridge. "A few days' march to the nearest border."

"Let's move," Daren says.

We follow Diane into the dense timber. As the steady rhythm of our boots settles against the earth and pine needles, the looming tension of Meridith, Eiden, and the coming war recedes into the quiet background.

My thoughts pull inward, centering on the faint, rhythmic pulse at my waist.

The magical stats are active. Real mana circulates through my limbs, and I know now that knowledge can be drawn from the Magium without madness or oblivion. Kate's suggestion—using Magium theorems to rework the stat booster into an interface that channels directly from the magical plane—is no longer a theorist's daydream.

The goal that has driven me since I was five years old is within reach.

Not impossible. Not a fantasy.

I can become a mage.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch11a-Credits, v_current_scene = B3-Ch11a-Credits)


ID: B3-Ch11a-Credits
TEXT:

Chapter 12 of Book 3 is now available. Press the button below to proceed.

